{
  "image_name": "3f480bd52fd7866590971f518fa0cbad.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how gut microbes and inflammation can drive carcinogenesis in the intestinal epithelium. Along the top, a progression is shown from non-transformed epithelial cells to cancer cells, with oxidative stress, microbial products, and bacterial toxins acting on the epithelial surface. Bacteria such as E. coli, Enterobacteria, and segmented filamentous bacteria appear to contribute through PAMP signaling, genotoxins, and induction of nitric oxide and reactive oxygen species, which damage epithelial cells and promote inflammatory signaling.\n\nThe central theme is a cytokine network linking epithelial cells, fibroblasts, monocytes, macrophages, dendritic cells, and lymphocytes. NF-κB is a major hub, activated in immune and epithelial compartments, leading to pro-IL-1β and NLRP3 activity and release of IL-1β. Additional cytokines, including IL-33, IL-23, TGFβ, IL-22, IL-6, IL-11, and IL-17A/F, connect innate and adaptive immune cells such as ILC3, γδ T cells, and TH17 cells.\n\nOn the tumor side, STAT3 and NF-κB signaling are highlighted in transformed epithelial cells. These pathways are associated with visible outcomes listed in the figure: proliferation, stemness, EMT, and migration. Overall, the image portrays microbiota-driven chronic inflammation as a mechanism that supports tumor initiation and progression.",
